    {
      "id": "r20261104-a02",
      "title": "The Quiet Rise of Repair Cafes",
      "level": "Menengah atas",
      "min": 4,
      "body": [
        "When your toaster breaks, what do you do? Most people throw it away and buy a new one. But in hundreds of cities, people now bring broken things to a 'repair cafe' instead.",
        "A repair cafe is a free meeting place, usually in a library or community center. Volunteers with repair skills fix your broken lamp, bicycle, or jacket while you watch and learn. The idea started in Amsterdam in 2009 and has spread to more than two thousand locations worldwide.",
        "Repair cafes are good for the environment because fewer things end up as waste. But visitors say the best part is something else: learning that a broken thing is not the end. Often, a ten-minute fix can give an object five more years of life, and give you a new skill for free."
      ],
      "gloss": {
        "toaster": "pemanggang roti",
        "throw away": "membuang",
        "volunteers": "relawan",
        "community": "komunitas",
        "spread": "menyebar",
        "waste": "limbah",
        "skill": "keterampilan"
      },
